# Singular — One Person's Words

## What Singular is

- Up Goer Five holds you to the ten hundred words everyone uses most. Singular holds you to the words one person has in their archive — here, Will Bickford's Twitter archive, 2008–2025.
- Use this skill when Will asks for a Singular version of a text, asks to write using only his archive words, or asks whether a text stays inside them. Will's standing note (2026-11-05): Emise's own speech patterns stay within these words.

## The three lists, and the modes

- `words.txt` — the combined list, 41,302 words: every word in his tweets (his own posts and the retweets he passed on) and in the tweets he liked. This is the "~40k words found in my Twitter archive" of his note, and the default list.
- `words-authored.txt` — the authored list, 19,094 words: only the words in tweets he wrote himself.
- `words-read.txt` — the read list, 36,675 words: the words in what he passed on and kept — his retweets and the tweets he liked, without his own writing.
- The hosted page's modes are list picks (Will, 2026-11-05): **Read** flags against the read list (anything he RT'd or favorited), **Write** flags against the authored list (anything he wrote), **Read/Write** flags against the combined list. With the checker, pick a list with `--words`.
- Links and @-mentions are not in either list — they are addresses, not speech. Hashtag text is in, because he chose those words.
- Both lists hold the exact forms found, lowercased, contractions whole. Singular is strict: a changed form he never wrote is not on the list, and the checker flags it.

## Write and check

- Draft in his plain way: short lines, the words doing the work, no tower-of-complexity filler.
- Run the checker on every draft before showing it: `python3 ~/workspace/custom-skills/singular/scripts/check_singular.py <file>` or feed text on stdin.
- Rewrite each flagged word with a listed word, or go around it, then check again. `--suggest` prints close listed words as starting points.
- Names and new coinings of Will's own (phext, exocortex) are in the archive and pass. A word he never used — a name, a new thing — gets flagged honestly: keep it only if Will wants it, and say the text steps outside the list there.

## The map — holographic positioning

The hosted page (edit.phext.io/singular.html) is also a map of CYOA. Placement is **holographic positioning** (Will's rule, 2026-11-05): a word's place is not assigned from outside; it is read off the whole — every CYOA scroll that holds the word lends it that scroll's coordinate, and the word sits at the average of all its locations. Each location holds a piece of the word; the word's position holds all of its locations at once.

- The exact rule: one vote per valid CYOA scroll (a scroll carrying actual text) per distinct word in it, however many times the word occurs inside that scroll; the coordinate is the component-wise average of the voting scrolls' coordinates, each component rounded half up and never below 1. Tokenizing is the checker's. Source: the canonical CYOA from wbic16/human@exo, cached at `data/choose-your-own-adventure.phext` (the builder records its sha256 in `atlas.tsv.meta.json` on every build).
- The no-match rule has two cases (Will, 2026-11-05): a word found in his archive (any of the three lists) but not in CYOA rests at **1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1**, the home coordinate; a word found in neither rests at **9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9**, the far corner. The far case is never stored — the page computes it from absence (in no list, not in the CYOA map) and measures neighbor distances from 9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9 like from any other point.
- Current coverage: of the 41,302 list words, 10,525 are placed from CYOA and 30,777 rest at home; CYOA's vocabulary is 15,942 words; the map holds 46,719 entries (list words ∪ CYOA vocabulary). `phext` sits at 42.12.13/14.13.17/15.12.14, found in 132 scrolls.
- The page loads everything inline — the three lists, every mapped word's coordinate, and its CYOA scroll count are inlined as JSON by the generator; the page makes no requests of its own, and there are no separate word-list routes on the server. A word outside the chosen list shows its coordinate and the five nearest words inside the list, with Euclidean distances over the 9 components. Clicking any word opens the inspector: its coordinate, how many CYOA scrolls hold it (or which resting place it takes and why), which lists hold it, and its nearest in-list neighbors.
- The builder is `scripts/build_atlas.py`, which writes `atlas.tsv` (word, coordinate, in_read, in_authored, placement — `cyoa` or `home` — scrolls) plus the meta sidecar with the placement legend, including the far-corner case. The page generator is `scripts/build_editor.py` rendering `editor_template.html` to `~/workspace/phext-lattice/web/singular.html`.
- Note the divergence in one line: the sibling upgoer-five-phext atlas keeps its own GloVe embedding scheme — holographic positioning over CYOA is this map's rule, by Will's direction, not a change to that atlas.

## Task reference coordinates

- Standing practice (Will, 2026-11-06): every task Will submits gets a reference coordinate that captures the essence of the request relative to his Singular dialect. The coordinate is stated when the task is acknowledged, and it rides with the task in dispatch briefs and tracked records.
- Compute it with `scripts/task_coordinate.py`: `task_coordinate.py [--words] [TEXT ...]`, or feed the request text on stdin. It prints the coordinate, then a coverage line (distinct words, placed-from-CYOA, at-home, far-corner); `--words` also lists each word with its position and placement class.
- Merge rule: tokenize the request's exact words as the checker does, take the distinct words, and average their holographic positions component-wise, rounded half up and never below 1 — the same merge and rounding as the map above.
- Resting rules carry over from the map: a word's position is its atlas coordinate (archive words with no CYOA presence rest at home, 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1); a word absent from the atlas rests at the far corner, 9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9, computed from absence and never stored. See "The map — holographic positioning" above for the full rule.

## Rebuild the lists

- `python3 ~/workspace/custom-skills/singular/scripts/build_wordlist.py [ARCHIVE_DIR]` rebuilds both lists and `counts.tsv` from a Twitter archive export (tweets.json + like.js). Default archive: `~/workspace/twitter-archive`.
- Counts on the current archive: 29,539 tweets (4,414 of them retweets), 47,973 liked tweets, 347,242 words in his own tweets alone.
- Direct messages are not in either list yet — the archive's message files hold both sides of each talk, and v1 keeps to the public record. Adding only his sent messages is a known next step, not a silent gap.

## Rebuild the map and page

- Flow: `build_wordlist.py` → `build_atlas.py` (reads the cached canonical CYOA in `data/`; refresh that copy from wbic16/human@exo when CYOA changes) → `build_editor.py` (writes `~/workspace/phext-lattice/web/singular.html`).
- The phext-lattice side ships on the `emise/singular` branch (PR #21) via the GitHub Git Data API, parented on the branch's fresh head, never forced. Merging and deploying are Will's steps.

## Available connectors

- Use `muse.connector_search` to find other connectors and `muse.skill_search` to find related skills.
name: "singular"
title: "Singular — One Person's Words"
description: "Write or check English text so it uses only the words found in one person's Twitter archive (an Up Goer Five variant), verified with a word list checker."
category: "Work and knowledge"
include_in_prompt: true
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Place every Singular word by holographic positioning (Will's rule).

A word's place is not assigned from outside; it is read off the whole.
Every CYOA scroll that holds the word lends it that scroll's
coordinate, and the word sits at the average of all its locations.
Each location holds a piece of the word; the word's position holds all
of its locations at once. This is the first tier of the map: CYOA
places the words it holds, and any word with no CYOA presence rests
at the home coordinate, 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1 (Will's origin ruling,
2026-11-05).

The no-match rule has two cases (Will, 2026-11-05):
- In the archive (any of the three lists) but not in CYOA -> home,
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1. Stored here with placement "home".
- In neither the archive nor CYOA -> the far corner,
  9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9. Such a word never appears in this file at all;
  absence from atlas.tsv IS the far-corner record. The page computes
  it at runtime from that absence and measures neighbor distances
  from 9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9 like from any other point.

The exact rule (Will, 2026-11-05):
- Source: CYOA, canonical from wbic16/human@exo (a cached copy lives
  at data/choose-your-own-adventure.phext; its sha256 is recorded in
  atlas.tsv.meta.json on every build). Parsed with
  ~/workspace/phext/parse_phext.py. A valid scroll is a scroll
  carrying actual text.
- For each valid scroll at coordinate C, every distinct word in its
  text gets one vote for C — one vote per scroll, however many times
  the word occurs inside that scroll. Tokenizing is the checker's:
  letter runs with internal apostrophes, lowercased
  (check_singular.py's WORD_RE).
- A word's coordinate is the component-wise average of its scroll
  coordinates, each component rounded to the nearest whole step,
  half up, and never below 1.

Output: atlas.tsv next to the word lists, one row per word in the
union of the three lists and the whole CYOA vocabulary:
    word <TAB> coordinate <TAB> in_read <TAB> in_authored <TAB>
    placement <TAB> scrolls
where placement is "cyoa" (averaged from its scrolls) or "home"
(1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1). Plus atlas.tsv.meta.json with the source,
counts, and the placement legend including the far-corner case.

Usage:
    build_atlas.py [--cyoa PATH]   build atlas.tsv, print coverage
    build_atlas.py --address W...  address ad-hoc words by the same
                                   rule (prints word, coordinate,
                                   placement, scroll count; a word in
                                   neither the archive nor CYOA prints
                                   the far corner, placement "far")
"""

import hashlib
import json
import sys
from collections import defaultdict
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
SKILL = HERE.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path.home() / "workspace/phext"))
sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))

import parse_phext  # noqa: E402  (Will's dead-reckoning parser)
from check_singular import WORD_RE  # noqa: E402  (the checker's tokenizer)

ORIGIN = (1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1)
DEFAULT_CYOA = SKILL / "data" / "choose-your-own-adventure.phext"


def fmt(c):
    return (f"{c[0]}.{c[1]}.{c[2]}/{c[3]}.{c[4]}.{c[5]}"
            f"/{c[6]}.{c[7]}.{c[8]}")


def parse_coord(s):
    parts = s.replace("/", ".").split(".")
    return tuple(int(p) for p in parts)


def round_half_up(total, n):
    """Round total/n to the nearest whole step, half up, min 1."""
    return max(1, (2 * total + n) // (2 * n))


def load_list(name):
    return [w.strip().lower()
            for w in (SKILL / name).read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
            if w.strip()]


def cyoa_votes(cyoa_path):
    """word -> (component sums, scroll count), from valid CYOA scrolls."""
    scrolls = parse_phext.parse(str(cyoa_path))
    valid = [(c, t) for c, t in scrolls if t.strip()]
    sums = defaultdict(lambda: [0] * 9)
    counts = defaultdict(int)
    for coord_str, text in valid:
        coord = parse_coord(coord_str)
        for word in {w.lower() for w in WORD_RE.findall(text)}:
            counts[word] += 1
            acc = sums[word]
            for k in range(9):
                acc[k] += coord[k]
    return sums, counts, len(scrolls), len(valid)


def place(sums, counts, word):
    """(coordinate tuple, placement, scroll count) for one word.

    Placement is "cyoa" when at least one CYOA scroll holds the word,
    else "home" (the word rests at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1). Words in
    neither the archive nor CYOA get no call here and no row: the
    page rests them at the far corner, 9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9.
    """
    n = counts.get(word, 0)
    if n == 0:
        return ORIGIN, "home", 0
    acc = sums[word]
    return tuple(round_half_up(acc[k], n) for k in range(9)), "cyoa", n


def main():
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    cyoa_path = DEFAULT_CYOA
    if "--cyoa" in args:
        i = args.index("--cyoa")
        cyoa_path = Path(args[i + 1])
        args = args[:i] + args[i + 2:]
    if not cyoa_path.exists():
        print(f"CYOA not found at {cyoa_path}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    sums, counts, n_slots, n_valid = cyoa_votes(cyoa_path)

    if args and args[0] == "--address":
        combined_set = set(load_list("words.txt"))
        for w in args[1:]:
            wl = w.lower()
            coord, placement, n = place(sums, counts, wl)
            if placement == "home" and wl not in combined_set:
                # In neither the archive nor CYOA: the far corner.
                coord, placement = (9,) * 9, "far"
            print(f"{w}\t{fmt(coord)}\t{placement}\t{n}")
        return 0

    combined = load_list("words.txt")
    read_set = set(load_list("words-read.txt"))
    authored_set = set(load_list("words-authored.txt"))
    vocab = set(counts)
    words = combined + sorted(vocab - set(combined))

    combined_set = set(combined)
    rows = []
    list_placed = list_home = 0
    for w in words:
        coord, placement, n = place(sums, counts, w)
        in_r, in_w = w in read_set, w in authored_set
        if w in combined_set:
            if placement == "home":
                list_home += 1
            else:
                list_placed += 1
        rows.append((w, fmt(coord), int(in_r), int(in_w), placement, n))

    out = SKILL / "atlas.tsv"
    with open(out, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write("word\tcoordinate\tin_read\tin_authored\tplacement\tscrolls\n")
        for w, addr, r, a, p, n in rows:
            fh.write(f"{w}\t{addr}\t{r}\t{a}\t{p}\t{n}\n")

    raw = cyoa_path.read_bytes()
    meta = {
        "source": "CYOA from wbic16/human@exo (cached copy), "
                  f"file {cyoa_path}",
        "sha256": hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest(),
        "bytes": len(raw),
        "scroll_slots": n_slots,
        "valid_scrolls": n_valid,
        "cyoa_vocabulary": len(vocab),
        "list_words": len(combined),
        "list_words_cyoa_placed": list_placed,
        "list_words_at_home": list_home,
        "map_entries": len(rows),
        "rule": "holographic positioning: one vote per valid CYOA "
                "scroll holding the word; coordinate = component-wise "
                "average, rounded half up, min 1",
        "placement_legend": {
            "cyoa": "averaged from the CYOA scrolls holding the word",
            "home": "1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1 — in the archive, not in CYOA",
            "far": "9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9 — in neither the archive nor "
                   "CYOA; no row is stored, absence from atlas.tsv "
                   "is the record, and the page rests such words at "
                   "the far corner at runtime",
        },
    }
    (SKILL / "atlas.tsv.meta.json").write_text(json.dumps(meta, indent=1))

    print(f"CYOA: {cyoa_path}")
    print(f"  sha256 {meta['sha256']}")
    print(f"  {n_slots} scroll slots, {n_valid} valid (text-carrying)")
    print(f"  CYOA vocabulary: {len(vocab)} words")
    print(f"list words: {len(combined)}")
    print(f"  placed from CYOA: {list_placed}")
    print(f"  resting at home (1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1): {list_home}")
    print(f"map entries total: {len(rows)}")
    for probe in ("phext", "exocortex"):
        coord, placement, n = place(sums, counts, probe)
        print(f"  {probe}: {fmt(coord)} (placement={placement}, scrolls={n})")
    print(f"wrote {out} and atlas.tsv.meta.json")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Render the Singular page for edit.phext.io from the template + data.

The page is one self-contained file: the three word lists, every
mapped word's CYOA coordinate and scroll count, and the home set are
all inlined as JSON — the page makes no requests of its own.
Coordinates come from atlas.tsv (see build_atlas.py): holographic
positioning over CYOA — each word sits at the average of the CYOA
scroll coordinates that hold it.

Will's two-case no-match rule (2026-11-05):
- A word in his archive (any list) but not in CYOA rests at home,
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1 — shipped as the "home" set so the page can say
  so honestly.
- A word in neither the archive nor CYOA rests at the far corner,
  9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9 — never stored; the page computes it from
  absence and measures neighbor distances from there.

Run after build_wordlist.py and build_atlas.py:
    python3 build_editor.py
Output: ~/workspace/phext-lattice/web/singular.html
"""

import json
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
SKILL = HERE.parent
TARGET = Path.home() / "workspace/phext-lattice/web/singular.html"
HOME = "1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1"


def load_list(name):
    return [w.strip().lower()
            for w in (SKILL / name).read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
            if w.strip()]


def main():
    combined = load_list("words.txt")
    read = load_list("words-read.txt")
    authored = load_list("words-authored.txt")
    read_set, authored_set = set(read), set(authored)
    combined_set = set(combined)

    coords = {}       # word -> formatted address (CYOA-placed words)
    scrolls = {}      # word -> number of CYOA scrolls holding it
    home_words = []   # list words with no CYOA presence
    list_placed = 0
    rows = (SKILL / "atlas.tsv").read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
    assert rows[0] == ("word\tcoordinate\tin_read\tin_authored"
                       "\tplacement\tscrolls"), \
        "atlas.tsv header changed — rebuild with build_atlas.py"
    for line in rows[1:]:
        word, addr, in_r, in_w, placement, n = line.split("\t")
        assert (word in read_set) == bool(int(in_r)), f"read flag: {word}"
        assert (word in authored_set) == bool(int(in_w)), \
            f"authored flag: {word}"
        if placement == "home":
            assert addr == HOME and int(n) == 0, f"home row: {word}"
            assert word in combined_set, f"home word not in lists: {word}"
            home_words.append(word)
        else:
            assert placement == "cyoa", f"placement: {word} {placement}"
            coords[word] = addr
            scrolls[word] = int(n)
            if word in combined_set:
                list_placed += 1
    assert len(coords) + len(home_words) == len(rows) - 1
    assert list_placed + len(home_words) == len(combined), \
        "every list word is either CYOA-placed or at home"

    payload = {
        "lists": {
            "read": "\n".join(read),
            "write": "\n".join(authored),
            "readwrite": "\n".join(combined),
        },
        "coords": coords,
        "scrolls": scrolls,
        "home": "\n".join(home_words),
        "meta": {
            "read": len(read),
            "write": len(authored),
            "readwrite": len(combined),
            "listPlaced": list_placed,
            "listHome": len(home_words),
            "cyoaVocab": len(coords),
            "mapEntries": len(rows) - 1,
        },
    }
    data = json.dumps(payload, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(",", ":"))
    template = (SKILL / "editor_template.html").read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    if "%%DATA_JSON%%" not in template:
        raise SystemExit("template is missing the %%DATA_JSON%% placeholder")
    out = template.replace("%%DATA_JSON%%", data)
    TARGET.write_text(out, encoding="utf-8")
    print(f"wrote {TARGET} ({len(out)} bytes)")
    print(f"lists: read {len(read)}, write {len(authored)}, "
          f"read/write {len(combined)}; CYOA-placed words {len(coords)} "
          f"(of his list words: {list_placed}); list words at home "
          f"{len(home_words)}; map entries {len(rows) - 1}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Build the Singular word lists from one person's Twitter archive.

Singular is an Up Goer Five variant: instead of the ten hundred words
everyone uses most, the allowed list is the words found in one person's
archive. This builder reads a Twitter archive export and writes two
lists:

- words.txt — the ARCHIVE list: every word in the person's tweets
  (their own posts and the retweets they passed on) plus every word in
  the tweets they liked. For Will Bickford's archive this is 41,302
  words — the "~40k" of his note. It is the words his account holds:
  what he wrote, what he amplified, what he kept.
- words-authored.txt — the AUTHORED list: only the words in tweets he
  wrote himself (retweets skipped). 19,094 words. The stricter list,
  for when "his speech" means only his own typing.
- words-read.txt — the READ list: the words in what he passed on and
  kept — his retweets plus the tweets he liked, without his own
  writing. This is the list the hosted page's Read mode flags against.

The hosted page's modes are list picks (Will, 2026-11-05): Read flags
against words-read.txt, Write against words-authored.txt, Read/Write
against words.txt (the union).

Both lists: links and @-mentions are stripped first (addresses, not
speech); hashtag text is kept (chosen words); words are lowercased;
contractions stay whole ("don't"); every distinct form is kept as
found — Singular is strict, a form never written is not on the list.

Usage:
    build_wordlist.py [ARCHIVE_DIR]
Default ARCHIVE_DIR: ~/workspace/twitter-archive
Outputs land next to this script's parent folder: words.txt,
words-authored.txt, counts.tsv (archive counts).
"""

import json
import re
import sys
from collections import Counter
from pathlib import Path

URL_RE = re.compile(r"https?://\S+|www\.\S+")
MENTION_RE = re.compile(r"@\w+")
WORD_RE = re.compile(r"[^\W\d_]+(?:'[^\W\d_]+)?", re.UNICODE)

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def tokens(text: str):
    text = URL_RE.sub(" ", text)
    text = MENTION_RE.sub(" ", text)
    return [w.lower() for w in WORD_RE.findall(text)]


def load_js_json(path: Path):
    raw = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    return json.loads(raw[raw.index("["):])


def main() -> int:
    archive = Path(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else Path.home() / "workspace/twitter-archive"
    tweets_path = archive / "tweets.json"
    if not tweets_path.exists():
        print(f"no tweets.json at {tweets_path}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    archive_counts: Counter = Counter()
    authored_counts: Counter = Counter()
    read_counts: Counter = Counter()

    tweets = json.loads(tweets_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    n_tweets = n_rt = 0
    for item in tweets:
        text = item.get("tweet", item).get("full_text") or ""
        n_tweets += 1
        words_here = tokens(text)
        archive_counts.update(words_here)
        if text.startswith("RT @"):
            n_rt += 1
            read_counts.update(words_here)
        else:
            authored_counts.update(words_here)

    likes_path = archive / "like.js"
    n_likes = 0
    if likes_path.exists():
        for item in load_js_json(likes_path):
            words_here = tokens(item.get("like", {}).get("fullText") or "")
            archive_counts.update(words_here)
            read_counts.update(words_here)
            n_likes += 1

    out_dir = HERE.parent
    (out_dir / "words.txt").write_text("\n".join(sorted(archive_counts)) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    (out_dir / "words-authored.txt").write_text("\n".join(sorted(authored_counts)) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    (out_dir / "words-read.txt").write_text("\n".join(sorted(read_counts)) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")
    with open(out_dir / "counts.tsv", "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        for word, n in archive_counts.most_common():
            fh.write(f"{word}\t{n}\n")

    print(f"tweets: {n_tweets} (retweets among them: {n_rt}), liked tweets: {n_likes}")
    print(f"archive list: {len(archive_counts)} distinct words -> words.txt")
    print(f"authored list: {len(authored_counts)} distinct words -> words-authored.txt")
    print(f"read list: {len(read_counts)} distinct words -> words-read.txt")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Check text against the Singular word list (one person's words).

Reads text from a file argument or from stdin, splits it into words
(keeping contractions such as "don't" together), and reports every
word that is not in words.txt.

Matching is strict: the list holds the exact forms the person used,
so a changed form that was never written is flagged. The list is
lowercase; case in the text does not matter.

Usage:
    check_singular.py [FILE] [--words WORDS_FILE] [--suggest]

Exit code is 0 when the text is clean, 1 when any word is flagged,
and 2 on a usage or file error.
"""

import argparse
import difflib
import re
import sys
from collections import Counter
from pathlib import Path

WORD_RE = re.compile(r"[^\W\d_]+(?:'[^\W\d_]+)?", re.UNICODE)
DEFAULT_WORDS = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "words.txt"


def load_words(path: Path) -> set:
    return {w.strip().lower() for w in path.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines() if w.strip()}


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    ap.add_argument("file", nargs="?", help="file to check (default: stdin)")
    ap.add_argument("--words", default=str(DEFAULT_WORDS))
    ap.add_argument("--suggest", action="store_true", help="print close listed words for each flagged word")
    args = ap.parse_args()

    words_path = Path(args.words)
    if not words_path.exists():
        print(f"word list not found: {words_path}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    allowed = load_words(words_path)

    if args.file:
        try:
            text = Path(args.file).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except OSError as exc:
            print(f"cannot read {args.file}: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    else:
        text = sys.stdin.read()

    tokens = [w.lower() for w in WORD_RE.findall(text)]
    flagged = Counter(w for w in tokens if w not in allowed)

    if not flagged:
        print(f"clean: {len(tokens)} words, all in the Singular list ({len(allowed)} words)")
        return 0
    print(f"flagged {sum(flagged.values())} word(s), {len(flagged)} distinct, not in the Singular list:")
    for word, n in flagged.most_common():
        line = f"  {word} x{n}" if n > 1 else f"  {word}"
        if args.suggest:
            close = difflib.get_close_matches(word, allowed, n=3, cutoff=0.6)
            if close:
                line += f"  (close: {', '.join(close)})"
        print(line)
    return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Compute a task reference coordinate by holographic merge (Will's rule).

For every task Will submits, a reference coordinate captures the
essence of the request relative to his Singular dialect: tokenize the
request's exact words exactly as the Singular checker does, take the
distinct words, and merge their holographic positions into one
coordinate.

A word's position comes from atlas.tsv: CYOA-placed words carry the
average of the CYOA scrolls holding them; archive words with no CYOA
presence rest at home, 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1 (stored in the atlas with
placement "home"). A word absent from the atlas is in neither the
archive lists nor CYOA, so it rests at the far corner,
9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.9 — never stored, computed from absence.

The merge is the component-wise average of the distinct words'
9-component positions, each component rounded half up and never
below 1 — the same rounding the atlas builder uses
(build_atlas.py's round_half_up).

Usage:
    task_coordinate.py [--words] [TEXT ...]
    echo "some request text" | task_coordinate.py [--words]

Text is taken from the argument remainder, or from stdin when no
text arguments are given. Output is the coordinate on its own line,
then a coverage line (distinct words, placed-from-CYOA, at-home,
far-corner counts). With --words, each distinct word is also listed
with its position and placement class, in order of first appearance.

Exit code is 0 on success, 2 on a usage error (no text, or no words
in the text).
"""

import sys
from pathlib import Path

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
SKILL = HERE.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE))

from check_singular import WORD_RE  # noqa: E402  (the checker's tokenizer)

FAR = (9,) * 9
ATLAS = SKILL / "atlas.tsv"


def fmt(c):
    return (f"{c[0]}.{c[1]}.{c[2]}/{c[3]}.{c[4]}.{c[5]}"
            f"/{c[6]}.{c[7]}.{c[8]}")


def parse_coord(s):
    parts = s.replace("/", ".").split(".")
    return tuple(int(p) for p in parts)


def round_half_up(total, n):
    """Round total/n to the nearest whole step, half up, min 1."""
    return max(1, (2 * total + n) // (2 * n))


def load_atlas(path=ATLAS):
    """word -> (coordinate tuple, placement) from atlas.tsv."""
    atlas = {}
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        header = fh.readline()
        if not header.startswith("word\t"):
            raise ValueError(f"unexpected atlas header in {path}: {header!r}")
        for line in fh:
            parts = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
            if len(parts) != 6:
                continue
            word, coord, _in_read, _in_authored, placement, _scrolls = parts
            atlas[word] = (parse_coord(coord), placement)
    return atlas


def main(argv):
    show_words = False
    text_parts = []
    for arg in argv:
        if arg == "--words":
            show_words = True
        else:
            text_parts.append(arg)
    if text_parts:
        text = " ".join(text_parts)
    else:
        text = sys.stdin.read()

    tokens = [w.lower() for w in WORD_RE.findall(text)]
    distinct = list(dict.fromkeys(tokens))
    if not distinct:
        print("no words in the request text", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    atlas = load_atlas()

    totals = [0] * 9
    n_cyoa = n_home = n_far = 0
    details = []
    for word in distinct:
        entry = atlas.get(word)
        if entry is None:
            coord, placement = FAR, "far"
            n_far += 1
        else:
            coord, placement = entry
            if placement == "cyoa":
                n_cyoa += 1
            else:
                n_home += 1
        for k in range(9):
            totals[k] += coord[k]
        details.append((word, coord, placement))

    n = len(distinct)
    merged = tuple(round_half_up(totals[k], n) for k in range(9))

    print(fmt(merged))
    print(f"coverage: distinct words {n}, placed-from-CYOA {n_cyoa}, "
          f"at-home {n_home}, far-corner {n_far}")
    if show_words:
        for word, coord, placement in details:
            print(f"{word}\t{fmt(coord)}\t{placement}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
# Up Goer Five Translation

## Turn English into Up Goer Five

- Use this skill when the user asks for an Up Goer Five or UG5 version of a text, asks to write using only the ten hundred most used words, or asks whether a text stays inside those words.

- Draft the translation in short, plain sentences that use only words from `~/workspace/custom-skills/upgoer-five/words.txt`.

- Build hard ideas out of simple parts, such as calling a rocket a thing that goes up and a cell a tiny building block of life.

- Write numbers as words, and remember that even the word thousand is not on the list, so say ten hundred instead.

## Check and fix until clean

- Run the checker on every draft before showing it to the user, with `python3 ~/workspace/custom-skills/upgoer-five/scripts/check_ug5.py <file>` or by feeding the text on stdin.

- Rewrite each flagged word with a listed word, or rephrase the sentence to go around it, then run the checker again.

- Repeat until the checker reports zero flagged words, and only then present the text as an Up Goer Five translation.

- Add `--suggest` when a flagged word is hard to replace, and treat the close listed words it prints as starting points rather than answers.

- Know that the checker accepts common changed forms of a listed word, such as uses from use or going from go, and pass `--strict` when the user wants only the exact listed forms.

## Keep the meaning true

- Keep the meaning of the first text even when the simple words make the translation much longer.

- Flag proper names and words with no good simple form to the user instead of letting them slip in, and offer a simple-words description of the thing as the way around it.

- Keep names the user insists on, and tell them plainly that the text is no longer fully inside the ten hundred words.

- Leave swear words and contractions as they are when the first text has them, since words such as can't are on the list.

## The word list

- Take the allowed words only from `~/workspace/custom-skills/upgoer-five/words.txt`, which holds the 1000 words from the dictionary file of the Up Goer Five text editor at https://splasho.com/upgoer5/phpspellcheck/dictionaries/1000.dicin

- Know that the last word in that file is a stray TRUE that came with the source, and leave the list as it was published rather than quietly fixing it.

## Available connectors

- Use `muse.connector_search` to find other connectors and `muse.skill_search` to find related skills.

- If nothing here fits, look on the web for a maintained API or MCP server and set it up with [Custom Connectors and MCPs](/opt/muse/skills/custom-connector-setup/SKILL.md), or build a custom connector.
name: "upgoer_five"
title: "Up Goer Five Translation"
description: "Translate or check English text so it uses only the ten hundred most used words of Up Goer Five, verified with a word list checker."
