# Ranch State Distillation — the ranch, sampled into state.phext

Will's design (2026-11-06): track the ranch state across time as **distillation only**. The purpose is biofeedback — the Exocortex is part of how Will's brain models itself, and a steady, distilled state signal is what lets him learn to read and steer it. Scheduled embodiment: cron `state-phext-sampler` (every 5 minutes, America/Chicago), which runs `scripts/state_sampler.py` and stays silent unless a cycle fails.

## The pipeline (one cycle, fully deterministic — no LLM in the loop)

1. **Pull.** One `ranch fanout` pass through the ranch proxy (`~/workspace/ranch-proxy/ranch`, the only sanctioned route to the fleet) over every enabled node: load, core count, top processes, ollama / render / gateway markers, GPU edge temperature. CPU Tctl comes from the fanout footers, which the proxy measures itself — including for nodes it thermally skips, so the hotspot is never invisible at the moment it matters most. Disabled nodes (Aurora Continuum, Best Willow, Delta Wood) are recorded as down from `nodes.json`; an enabled node that refuses auth (Uncanny Valley — jared's key grant is outstanding) is recorded as no-access, never as down.
2. **Summarize.** A template composes one or two lines of plain language: nodes up out of 17, the busy set (render / inference / compute, by node) or "fleet idle", average Tctl across the nodes reporting, the hotspot (flagged when it crosses the 75 °C sustained cap), a GPU hotspot clause only at 65 °C or above, then the down and no-access lists. A node is busy when a render marker is live, when ollama is up under load (≥ 0.5), or when load reaches three quarters of its cores.
3. **Coordinate.** The summary's holographic coordinate is computed with the Singular skill's own machinery — `check_singular.WORD_RE` tokenization (letters only; digits carry no words) and `task_coordinate`'s atlas load and component-wise merge (round half up, never below 1) — imported, never reimplemented. The timestamp is NOT part of the merged text: a state that reads the same distills to the same coordinate whenever it recurs.
4. **Store (WAL by construction — Will's ruling 2026-11-09, stated 01:42 and corrected by him minutes later).** Keep a minimum coordinate — the **floor**, the highest coordinate already written in `state.phext` — and enforce that every new write lands **strictly above it**. Previously written scrolls are immutable, and that is what makes the WAL automatic: phext serializes scrolls in coordinate order, so a strictly-greater write appends its delimiter delta and text at the file's tail and touches no byte already written (verifiable: after any cycle, the old file is a byte-exact prefix of the new one). Coordinate assignment is **indirect**: the summary's holographic coordinate is the entry's logical address; when the floor displaces the write to a higher physical coordinate, the stored line is prefixed with the logical coordinate (`logical | YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM TZ | summary`), and readers resolve the logical view by folding the physical scrolls in write order (`read_state_scrolls()` in the sampler). An occupied logical coordinate gains a line; nothing is ever overwritten or dropped. Why not the original parse → modify → implode → atomic-replace: the Muse VM substrate denies whole-file temp+rename writes (Errno 1 on the temp write; appends pass) — a substrate failure logged in the qexo design notes (EVALUATIONS.md, 2026-11-09). (An earlier same-night implementation appended to a separate `state.wal` file; Will corrected the reading — the WAL is a property of the coordinate discipline inside state.phext itself, not a sidecar file. The sidecar's two entries were migrated in under the floor discipline and the file retired.)

## What never enters the phext

Raw pulls live outside, as working state: `~/workspace/state-sampler/hidden_files/last-pull.json` (latest pull, overwritten) and `pulls.jsonl` (size-capped log). They are debugging surface only. If a fact matters long-term it will arrive as a summary line or not at all — that restraint is the design: the phext stays small and reviewable for years.

## Reading state.phext

- The physical file holds scrolls in write order at monotonically increasing coordinates; the **logical** view folds them: a line with an indirect prefix belongs to the logical coordinate it names, any other line to the physical scroll holding it. The sampler's `read_state_scrolls()` performs the fold; a raw parse (`~/workspace/phext/parse_phext.py state.phext`) shows the physical layout, whose high-coordinate tail is the displaced recent entries.
- Home (1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1) documents the layout and entry format. In the logical view, every scroll is one distilled state with its occurrence lines; Home's own prose lines fold to Home, not to the state scrolls.
- Scrolls cluster by resemblance in logical space — a changed hotspot or busy set moves the logical coordinate slightly; a change of a degree or a count does not move it at all (numbers ride in the line text). The logical map is the map of the shapes the ranch takes over time.
- Verify any entry's logical placement by re-running the singular `task_coordinate.py` on its summary text — the coordinate must match the recorded logical address exactly.

## Failure discipline

- One dead node never stalls a cycle: the fanout is parallel and timeout-bounded, and unreachable nodes are summarized as down / no-access, not retried.
- A cycle that cannot append leaves the phext untouched and exits nonzero; the cron reports only failures, briefly, and never edits the phext by hand or improvises a repair.
- Growth is linear in lines (288 pulls/day): roughly 53 KB/day of line text plus small delimiter overhead per displaced scroll — about 1.6 MB at 30 days, 5 MB at 90. If that ever needs trimming, the levers are cadence and line length, chosen by Will — never silent pruning of the record.
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Ranch state sampler: one 5-minute cycle of the state.phext distillation.

Pipeline (Will's design, 2026-11-06):
  1. Probe the ranch fleet through the ranch proxy (`ranch fanout`) — one
     parallel pass over every enabled node collecting load, core count,
     the top processes, ollama / render / gateway markers, and GPU edge
     temperature. CPU Tctl comes from the fanout footers, which the proxy
     measures itself (and reports even for thermally skipped nodes).
  2. Compose a one-or-two-line plain-language summary of the cluster —
     deterministic template, no LLM in the loop.
  3. Compute the summary's holographic coordinate by Will's Singular
     method, reusing the singular skill's own machinery (check_singular's
     WORD_RE tokenizer + task_coordinate's atlas load and merge) — never
     reimplemented here.
  4. Append the summary into state.phext under the WAL discipline
     (Will's ruling, 2026-11-09, as he corrected it the same night):
     keep a minimum coordinate — the floor, the highest coordinate
     already written — and enforce that every new write lands strictly
     above it. Previously written scrolls are immutable, so the WAL is
     automatic: phext serializes scrolls in coordinate order, hence a
     strictly-greater write appends bytes at the file's tail and
     touches nothing already written. Coordinate assignment is
     indirect: the summary's holographic coordinate is its logical
     address; when the floor forces a higher physical coordinate, the
     stored line records the logical coordinate first, and readers
     resolve logical → entries by folding the file in write order.
     An occupied logical coordinate gains a line; nothing is
     overwritten, nothing is dropped. The substrate failure that
     forced the design (the Muse VM denies whole-file temp+rename
     writes; appends pass) is logged in the qexo design notes
     (EVALUATIONS.md, 2026-11-09). The phext holds ONLY distilled
     summaries — raw pulls live in the working-state dir below, never
     in the phext.

Working state (outside the phext): ~/workspace/state-sampler/hidden_files/
  last-pull.json  — the most recent raw pull (overwritten each cycle)
  pulls.jsonl     — raw pull log, size-capped (rotated, newest kept)

Usage: state_sampler.py [--dry-run]   (--dry-run probes + prints, writes nothing)
Exit code: 0 on success (including a lock-skip), 1 on a hard failure.
"""

import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo

HOME_DIR = Path.home()
RANCH = HOME_DIR / "workspace/ranch-proxy/ranch"
NODES_JSON = HOME_DIR / "workspace/ranch-proxy/nodes.json"
PHEXT_DIR = HOME_DIR / "workspace/phext"
STATE_PHEXT = PHEXT_DIR / "state.phext"
WORK_DIR = HOME_DIR / "workspace/state-sampler/hidden_files"
SINGULAR_SCRIPTS = HOME_DIR / "workspace/custom-skills/singular/scripts"
TZ = ZoneInfo("America/Chicago")
HOME_COORD = (1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1)
THERMAL_CAP_C = 75  # Will's sustained Tctl target; flag crossings in the summary

sys.path.insert(0, str(SINGULAR_SCRIPTS))
sys.path.insert(0, str(PHEXT_DIR))
from check_singular import WORD_RE          # noqa: E402  (the Singular tokenizer)
from task_coordinate import load_atlas, round_half_up, fmt  # noqa: E402
from parse_phext import parse as parse_phext  # noqa: E402
from build_skills_phext import implode, LEVEL_BYTES  # noqa: E402

DELIM_BYTES = {0x01, 0x1F, 0x1E, 0x1D, 0x1C, 0x1A, 0x19, 0x18, 0x17}

PROBE = (
    "load=$(cut -d' ' -f1 /proc/loadavg); echo load=$load; "
    "echo ncpu=$(nproc); "
    "ps -eo pcpu=,comm= --sort=-pcpu | head -3 | "
    "awk '{s=s $2 \":\" int($1+0.5) \" \"} END {print \"top=\" s}'; "
    "pgrep -x ollama >/dev/null && echo ollama=up || echo ollama=off; "
    "pgrep -f 'chunk_rende[r]|ffmpe[g]|blende[r]' >/dev/null && echo render=on || echo render=off; "
    "pgrep -f 'opencla[w]' >/dev/null && echo gateway=on || echo gateway=off; "
    "for d in /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*; do "
    "if grep -qx amdgpu $d/name 2>/dev/null; then "
    "for f in $d/temp*_label; do "
    "if grep -qx edge $f 2>/dev/null; then ti=${f%_label}_input; "
    "echo gpu=$(( $(cat $ti 2>/dev/null) / 1000 )); fi; done; fi; done; true"
)

HOME_TEXT = """Home — state.phext: the ranch, distilled
kind: observed | origin: Muse (this agent) | maintained by: the state-phext-sampler cron (every 5 minutes) running ~/workspace/custom-skills/ranch-state-distillation/scripts/state_sampler.py
source of truth: this file. Raw pulls are working state only, kept outside the phext at ~/workspace/state-sampler/hidden_files/ — never inscribed here.

What this is: every 5 minutes the sampler probes the ranch fleet (17 nodes) through the ranch proxy, writes a one-or-two-line plain-language summary of the cluster (nodes up and down, what is running where, average CPU Tctl, the hotspot), computes the summary's holographic coordinate by Will's Singular method, and stores ONLY the distilled summary as a scroll at that coordinate. No raw telemetry lives in this phext; it stays small and reviewable long-term.

Layout: this Home scroll sits at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1. Every other scroll is one distilled state of the ranch. Its coordinate is the holographic merge of the summary's words — Singular tokenization (letters only; digits carry no words), the component-wise average of each distinct word's atlas position, rounded half up and never below 1, exactly the task-coordinate method — computed over the summary text WITHOUT its timestamp, so a state that reads the same distills to the same coordinate whenever it recurs.

Entry format: one line per pull — "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM TZ | summary". When a new summary computes to a coordinate already occupied, its line is appended inside that scroll with its own timestamp; scrolls are never overwritten and lines are never dropped. Numbers (temperatures, counts) ride inside the line text; the coordinate merges words only, so states differing only in a degree or a count share a scroll, while a changed hotspot or a changed busy set moves the coordinate. If a state ever computed to the Home coordinate itself, it would be stored one scroll past it, at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.2, with its true coordinate noted in the line — the meta line is reserved.

Reading it: scrolls cluster by resemblance — near-identical states sit at near-identical coordinates, so the phext reads as a map of the shapes the ranch takes over time.
"""


def probe_fleet():
    """One ranch fanout pass. Returns {node_name: result-dict} for every
    roster node (enabled or not)."""
    roster = json.loads(NODES_JSON.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))["nodes"]
    results = {}
    for node in roster:
        results[node["name"]] = {
            "name": node["name"], "enabled": bool(node.get("enabled")),
            "status": "down" if not node.get("enabled") else "unreachable",
            "tctl": None, "load": None, "ncpu": None, "top": "",
            "ollama": None, "render": None, "gateway": None, "gpu": None,
        }
    proc = subprocess.run(
        [str(RANCH), "fanout", PROBE],
        capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=240,
    )
    out = proc.stdout or ""
    blocks = re.split(r"^===== (.+?) =====$", out, flags=re.M)
    # blocks: [prelude, name, body, name, body, ...]
    for i in range(1, len(blocks) - 1, 2):
        name, body = blocks[i].strip(), blocks[i + 1]
        if name not in results:
            continue
        r = results[name]
        m = re.search(r"^----- rc=(-?\d+) \d+ms Tctl=([0-9.]+|None|-)$", body, flags=re.M)
        skipped = re.search(r"^SKIPPED \(([^)]*?)(?:, Tctl ([0-9.]+)C)?\)$", body, flags=re.M)
        if skipped:
            r["status"] = "thermal-hold"
            if skipped.group(2):
                r["tctl"] = float(skipped.group(2))
            continue
        if m:
            if m.group(2) not in ("-", "None"):
                r["tctl"] = float(m.group(2))
            if int(m.group(1)) != 0:
                r["status"] = "no-access" if "Permission denied" in body else "unreachable"
                continue
            r["status"] = "up"
        else:
            r["status"] = "unreachable"
            continue
        kv = dict(re.findall(r"^(\w+)=(\S.*)$", body, flags=re.M))
        if "load" in kv:
            try:
                r["load"] = float(kv["load"])
            except ValueError:
                pass
        if "ncpu" in kv:
            try:
                r["ncpu"] = int(kv["ncpu"])
            except ValueError:
                pass
        r["top"] = kv.get("top", "").strip()
        r["ollama"] = kv.get("ollama")
        r["render"] = kv.get("render")
        r["gateway"] = kv.get("gateway")
        if "gpu" in kv:
            try:
                r["gpu"] = int(kv["gpu"])
            except ValueError:
                pass
    return results


def classify(r):
    """(busy?, activity-label) for one probed node."""
    if r["status"] == "thermal-hold":
        return (False, "thermal-hold")
    if r["status"] != "up":
        return (False, r["status"])
    load = r["load"] or 0.0
    if r["render"] == "on":
        return (True, "render")
    if r["ollama"] == "up" and load >= 0.5:
        return (True, "inference")
    if r["ncpu"] and load >= 0.75 * r["ncpu"]:
        return (True, "compute")
    if load >= 2.0:
        return (True, "busy")
    return (False, "idle")


def summarize(results, total):
    ups = [r for r in results.values() if r["status"] in ("up", "thermal-hold")]
    downs = sorted(r["name"] for r in results.values() if r["status"] == "down")
    noacc = sorted(r["name"] for r in results.values()
                   if r["status"] in ("no-access", "unreachable"))
    busy = []
    for r in sorted(ups, key=lambda x: x["name"]):
        is_busy, label = classify(r)
        if is_busy:
            busy.append((label, r["name"]))
    temps = {r["name"]: r["tctl"] for r in ups if r["tctl"] is not None}
    clauses = [f"{len(ups)}/{total} nodes up"]
    if busy:
        shown = busy[:3]
        clause = "busy: " + ", ".join(f"{label} on {name}" for label, name in shown)
        if len(busy) > 3:
            clause += f", +{len(busy) - 3} more"
        clauses.append(clause)
    else:
        clauses.append("fleet idle")
    if temps:
        avg = round(sum(temps.values()) / len(temps))
        clauses.append(f"avg Tctl {avg}C across {len(temps)} reporting")
        hot_name, hot_t = max(temps.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1])
        hot_clause = f"hotspot {hot_name} {round(hot_t)}C"
        if hot_t >= THERMAL_CAP_C:
            hot_clause += f" (over the {THERMAL_CAP_C}C cap)"
        clauses.append(hot_clause)
    gpus = {r["name"]: r["gpu"] for r in ups if r.get("gpu") is not None}
    if gpus:
        g_name, g_t = max(gpus.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1])
        if g_t >= 65:
            clauses.append(f"gpu hotspot {g_name} {g_t}C")
    if downs:
        clauses.append("down: " + ", ".join(downs))
    if noacc:
        clauses.append("no-access: " + ", ".join(noacc))
    return "; ".join(clauses) + "."


def coordinate_of(text, atlas):
    tokens = [w.lower() for w in WORD_RE.findall(text)]
    distinct = list(dict.fromkeys(tokens))
    if not distinct:
        raise ValueError("summary produced no words")
    totals = [0] * 9
    for word in distinct:
        entry = atlas.get(word)
        coord = entry[0] if entry is not None else (9,) * 9
        for k in range(9):
            totals[k] += coord[k]
    n = len(distinct)
    return tuple(round_half_up(totals[k], n) for k in range(9))


def clean(text):
    return "".join(ch for ch in text if ord(ch) not in DELIM_BYTES)


def _parse_coord(s):
    return tuple(int(x) for x in s.replace("/", ".").split("."))


def _delta_bytes(state, target):
    """Delimiter bytes that move the implode state machine from `state`
    to `target` (target strictly greater), exactly as implode emits."""
    out = bytearray()
    st = list(state)
    for lvl in range(9):
        while st[lvl] < target[lvl]:
            out.append(LEVEL_BYTES[lvl])
            st[lvl] += 1
            for j in range(lvl + 1, 9):
                st[j] = 1
    assert tuple(st) == tuple(target), (state, target)
    return bytes(out)


def _floor_and_state():
    """The floor: the highest coordinate already written in
    state.phext, and the physical scrolls in write order."""
    if STATE_PHEXT.exists():
        physical = [(c, t) for c, t in parse_phext(str(STATE_PHEXT))]
    else:
        physical = []
    floor = HOME_COORD
    for coord_str, _ in physical:
        c = _parse_coord(coord_str)
        if c > floor:
            floor = c
    return floor, physical


def _append_line(logical, line):
    """Append `line` for logical coordinate `logical` to state.phext,
    enforcing the WAL discipline: the physical write lands strictly
    above the floor, so its bytes append at the tail and no written
    scroll is touched. When the floor displaces the write, the stored
    line is prefixed with the logical coordinate (indirect
    addressing); resolution folds by it. Returns the physical
    coordinate used."""
    floor, physical = _floor_and_state()
    have_home = any(_parse_coord(c) == HOME_COORD for c, _ in physical)
    if logical > floor:
        physical_coord = logical
    else:
        physical_coord = floor[:8] + (floor[8] + 1,)
    stored = line if physical_coord == logical else f"{fmt(logical)} | {line}"
    payload = bytearray()
    state = HOME_COORD
    if not have_home:
        payload += HOME_TEXT.encode()
        state = HOME_COORD
    else:
        state = floor
    payload += _delta_bytes(state, physical_coord)
    payload += (stored + "\n").encode()
    with open(STATE_PHEXT, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(payload.decode("utf-8"))
        fh.flush()
        os.fsync(fh.fileno())
    return physical_coord


def append_entry(summary, coord, timestamp):
    """Append one cycle entry under the WAL discipline. The summary's
    holographic coordinate is its logical address; the write itself
    always lands above the floor. Returns the physical coordinate."""
    line = f"{timestamp} | {clean(summary)}"
    return _append_line(coord, line)


_INDIRECT_RE = re.compile(r"^(\d+\.\d+\.\d+/\d+\.\d+\.\d+/\d+\.\d+\.\d+) \| (.*)$")


def read_state_scrolls():
    """Resolve the logical view: fold the physical scrolls in write
    order — a line carrying an indirect prefix belongs to the logical
    coordinate it names; any other line belongs to the physical scroll
    holding it."""
    _, physical = _floor_and_state()
    scrolls = {}
    for coord_str, text in physical:
        for raw in text.splitlines():
            m = _INDIRECT_RE.match(raw)
            if m:
                logical, line = m.group(1), m.group(2)
            else:
                logical, line = coord_str, raw
            if logical in scrolls and scrolls[logical].strip():
                scrolls[logical] = scrolls[logical].rstrip("\n") + "\n" + line
            else:
                scrolls[logical] = line
    scrolls.setdefault(fmt(HOME_COORD), HOME_TEXT)
    return scrolls


def save_raw(pull, keep_bytes=2_000_000):
    WORK_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    (WORK_DIR / "last-pull.json").write_text(
        json.dumps(pull, indent=1), encoding="utf-8")
    log = WORK_DIR / "pulls.jsonl"
    with open(log, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(pull) + "\n")
    if log.stat().st_size > keep_bytes:
        lines = log.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
        log.write_text("\n".join(lines[-1000:]) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")


def main(argv):
    dry = "--dry-run" in argv
    WORK_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    lock = open(WORK_DIR / "sampler.lock", "w")
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except OSError:
        print("skipped: previous cycle still running")
        return 0
    now = datetime.now(TZ)
    results = probe_fleet()
    total = len(results)
    summary = summarize(results, total)
    atlas = load_atlas()
    coord = coordinate_of(summary, atlas)
    timestamp = now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M %Z")
    pull = {"ts": now.isoformat(), "summary": summary,
            "coordinate": fmt(coord),
            "nodes": sorted(results.values(), key=lambda r: r["name"])}
    if dry:
        print(json.dumps(pull, indent=1))
        return 0
    used = append_entry(summary, coord, timestamp)
    save_raw(pull)
    print(f"{fmt(used)} | {timestamp} | {summary}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
# Mutable Upstream Sync — a two-band patch stack that tracks upstream nightly

How Emise keeps wbic16/mutable-os in sync with its upstream, argimenes/mutable-os (Will directed the pipeline 2026-11-06: "we need to get a pipeline going for staying in sync with upstream; the SSO changes we apply need to sit in a separate repo or branch; once phext support is merged upstream, we won't have to merge as frequently"). Standing invariant (Will, 2026-11-08): **"keeping sso handling downstream always"** — the SSO band is only ever rebased on top of the phext band; it never enters `emise/phext-support` and never rides an upstream submission. Companion to fork-patch-discipline; tracked under goal `mutable-phext-io-mutable-os-fork-setup-with-phext-patches`.

## The shape

Our divergence from upstream is a **two-band patch stack**, never a snowflake tree:

- **Phext band** — branch `emise/phext-support`. The phext support stack: the support squash (originally `64f8eea2209515d5f0fcae9766c2c79a5008a227`), per-Cavern mode selection, the font-settings domain, and the PATCHES.md record commit. This band is **upstream-bound**: it exists to be merged upstream one day, and it dissolves the day phext support lands there. Since the 2026-11-08 promotion it sits on genuine upstream `db3ed30064f07ba99784a1d395ba127787274501`; its current commit list is the one in `sync-bands.conf`.
- **SSO band** — branch `emise/sso`. The edit.phext.io SSO gate commit and the SSO UX-fixes commit. This band is **ours and never upstream-bound**; it stays separable from the phext band precisely so the phext band can go upstream (or dissolve) without dragging our site auth with it. Per the standing invariant, it is only ever rebased on top of the phext band — never merged into it, never part of an upstream submission.
- **`main`** is the integration/deploy line. It advances **by merge only** — main is never rebased and never force-pushed.

**Band purity is checkable, and is checked:** the phext-band diff (base..`emise/phext-support`) must contain zero SSO files and zero SSO content. If any SSO path or assertion/session code appears in that diff, the stack is contaminated — stop and rebuild the bands rather than syncing a tainted phext band. The SSO band's diff, by contrast, sits entirely downstream: base of the SSO band is always the phext band's tip.

Retired branches (2026-11-08 cleanup): `emise/sso-edit-v2` and `emise/sso-ux-fixes` were merged and deleted; `emise/sso-edit` is kept as provenance for the original pre-lift SSO commit. The live band branches are exactly two: `emise/phext-support` and `emise/sso`, plus dated `emise/backup/<date>-*` refs left by promotions.

The live band definition — the recorded base (`OLD_BASE`), the exact band commit lists, and the upstream-support marker files — is `sync-bands.conf` in the pipeline directory (below). The conf is the record; this skill describes the process around it. Marker files whose presence in the upstream tip tree means phext support has landed upstream: `server/phext-fs.mjs`, `src/phext/codec.ts`, `PHEXT.md`.

## Where everything lives

- Pipeline: `~/workspace/goals/mutable-phext-io-mutable-os-fork-setup-with-phext-patches/hidden_files/sync-pipeline/`
  - `upstream-sync.sh` — the trial sync (fetch, measure, trial-rebase, verify, report). Never pushes.
  - `promote.sh` — the promotion step. **A draft, guarded to exit 64 as a no-op**; see Promotion.
  - `sync-bands.conf` — band definition (above).
  - `recorded-set.txt` — the recorded vitest file set for verification.
  - `reports/` — one dated report per run (`sync-<stamp>.md`); `logs/<stamp>/` — per-suite logs.
  - `scratch/` — the pipeline's **dedicated scratch clone** of the fork. The sync never operates on the main clone (`~/workspace/mutable-os-work`) and refuses to run if the scratch path resolves to it.
  - One level up, in the goal's `hidden_files/`: `cleanup-promote.sh` — the script of record for the executed 2026-11-08 promotion (see Promotion).
- Publication routes: outbound git push from the Muse VM is blocked. Additive GitHub writes go through the API tooling — the goal's `hidden_files/publish_branch.py` / `publish_rebase.py` and `python3 ~/workspace/custom-connectors/custom.github/bin/github_api.py` — which parent on the branch's fresh remote HEAD with force disabled and abort on a moved HEAD. **But the API cannot create a re-anchored promotion lineage**: its parent objects live in upstream, not in the fork, so the Git Data API has nothing to parent the new band commits on. Promotion therefore runs from **aletheia-core via git-over-SSH** (`git@github.com`, authenticated as wbic16) — see Promotion; script of record: the goal's `hidden_files/cleanup-promote.sh`.

## The nightly trial — upstream-sync.sh

Scheduled embodiment: cron `mutable-upstream-sync`, daily ~03:44 America/Chicago, owned by the mutable goal. The script, in order:

1. Fetches origin (the fork) and upstream into the scratch clone.
2. Measures drift between `OLD_BASE` and the upstream default-branch tip: merge-base, commit counts on both sides, **content drift** (tree diff), and the touched-file overlap of the upstream changes against each band's file set.
3. **Twin check:** if `OLD_BASE` is not an ancestor of the upstream tip, runs a `range-diff` patch-twin check before treating the gap as drift (see the twin-lineage lesson).
4. **Endgame check:** if any support marker file exists in the upstream tip tree, stops with the mode-switch exit — no rebase attempted.
5. Trial-rebases the phext band (`git rebase --onto <tip> <OLD_BASE> <band tip>`), then cherry-picks the SSO band commits, in a throwaway worktree of the scratch clone.
6. On a clean stack: installs dependencies, then verifies **from a fresh build** (below), and writes the dated report, including the proposed post-promotion `sync-bands.conf` values and the resulting stack SHAs.

Exit codes — the cron run reports to Will on everything except 0:

| Code | Meaning | Cron behaviour |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | No drift — upstream tip is exactly the recorded base | Silent |
| 10 | Clean sync, fully verified | Message: new stack SHAs + verification tallies; promotion has NOT been done |
| 11 | Phext support detected upstream — mode switch needed | Message prominently: the support band can dissolve; re-plan as SSO-only |
| 20 | Rebase/cherry-pick conflict | Message with the conflicted files named; never auto-resolve |
| 30 | Build or test failure | Message with the failing tallies |
| 40 | Setup/environment error (fetch, install, bad config) | Message with the error (but see the install fallback) |

Every run appends a one-line outcome note to the mutable goal's GOAL.md state log.

## Verification discipline

- **Fresh build first**: `npm run build`, then typecheck. A suite tally from a stale `dist/` proves nothing.
- **Suites run serially** (`--no-file-parallelism`): the shared establishment flock makes parallel suites fail spuriously or hang.
- The battery: SSO + startup suites; the recorded vitest set (`recorded-set.txt`); `server/phext-settings.test.mjs` qualified separately under `node --test` (it fails vitest *collection* at baseline with zero tests — a failed-file list containing only it is a pass); `npm run test:sqlite`.
- **Install fallback:** if `npm install` fails on native-module downloads through the egress proxy (seen 2026-11-06 with better-sqlite3), the script may copy a `node_modules` whose tree's `package-lock.json` blob is identical to the trial tree's — same lock, same dependency bits — and verifies better-sqlite3 loads. No lock-identical donor, no fallback: exit 40.

## The twin-lineage lesson

A recorded base SHA that is **not an ancestor** of the upstream tip is not necessarily drift. Upstream history gets rewritten (the 2026-11-06 rebase produced exactly this): the first verified run measured base `4bf3a936` against tip `db3ed300` — not an ancestor, 15 commits on each side of the merge-base — yet the range-diff showed **15 of 15 patch-identical twins and zero content drift**. Prove twin-identity with `range-diff` / an empty tree diff before treating a non-ancestor base as divergence; ancestry is a claim about history, content is the fact.

## The endgame — mode switch (exit 11)

When phext support merges upstream, the marker files appear in the upstream tip tree and the sync stops asking for a full-band trial. A human then re-plans: retire the phext band, rewrite `sync-bands.conf` by hand as an SSO-only stack on the new base, and the nightly sync continues at a lower merge cadence — the SSO band is small and touches few files upstream moves often. The script never performs the mode switch itself.

## Promotion — separate, guarded; first run executed under per-sync direction

`upstream-sync.sh` prepares and proves; it **never pushes**. Publishing a verified trial is a separate step. `promote.sh` in the pipeline directory remains a **draft with a hard exit-64 guard** for a future unattended path — it must not be run, and the guard is removed only in a reviewed change, never edited around ad hoc. The first promotion was instead executed in session on 2026-11-08 (below), under Will's per-task direction, via the SSH route.

Why the split (Will's force-push rules, 2026-10-30 / 2026-11-06): fetch and rebase before any push, never force push — with one sanctioned exception, a rebase Will asks for, force-pushed `--force-with-lease` (never bare `--force`) with the old history preserved on a backup branch first, unless the rewrite would impact anyone except us. A sync promotion contains exactly one force-class operation (rewriting the two `emise/*` band branches), so it stays isolated, named, and gated.

When enabled for a specific verified report, promotion does:

0. **Preconditions, all before any write:** the report says CLEAN SYNC, VERIFIED and names the new stack; **no open PR has head `emise/phext-support` or `emise/sso`** (checked via the GitHub API) — if one is open, STOP and report; remote main and band heads still sit where the trial measured them (the lease-equivalent parent check).
1. **Backups first:** create dated backup refs at the current heads before any rewrite (additive) — the 2026-11-08 form: `emise/backup/2026-11-08-phext-support` at the old phext tip and `emise/backup/2026-11-08-sso-and-main` at the old SSO/main tip.
2. **Rebuild and publish the bands from aletheia-core, git-over-SSH** (`git@github.com` as wbic16): in a scratch clone on the node, cherry-pick the band commits onto the new upstream base, then **verify tree identity before any push** — the rebuilt stack tip's tree must equal main's tree, and the rebuilt phext band's tree must equal the prior band's tree (the re-anchor changes ancestry, never content). Only then push: `--force-with-lease` on `emise/phext-support` and `emise/sso` only. (The API route cannot do this step — the new lineage's parents are upstream objects; see Where everything lives.)
3. Advance `main` **by merge only**: merges API, base `main`, head `emise/sso`.
4. Verify: `ls-remote` all three refs; main's tree must equal the trial stack's tip tree from the report.
5. Update `sync-bands.conf` with the report's proposed values; append the promotion record to the goal's GOAL.md.

**First promotion — executed 2026-11-08** under Will's per-task direction ("work on cleaning up the integration, keeping sso handling downstream always"). This was per-sync approval exercised, **not** standing ratification. Shape and result: backups `emise/backup/2026-11-08-phext-support` @ `4f5ddaf27` and `emise/backup/2026-11-08-sso-and-main` @ `2b9273468` created first; the stack rebuilt by cherry-pick from aletheia-core and tree-verified before pushing; `--force-with-lease` on `emise/phext-support` and `emise/sso` only; `main` advanced by merge. The bands were re-anchored from the twin base `4bf3a936` onto genuine upstream `db3ed30064f07ba99784a1d395ba127787274501`: `emise/phext-support` @ `7b0e143d01d6568cb429a5a47f390f7bd57bbd42` (band `8f382ca0` / `d68fba1f` / `75d2571c` / `7b0e143d`), `emise/sso` @ `df424f3ab63a90dcb5c522ab5dd8d0313636f442` (SSO band `96259b14` / `df424f3ab`), `main` merged to `acb8130d32a1f80df9ea224aa59b463534db2f15` — with the tree byte-identical (`7c7639553a904f2fd1f8a7250a29262d5ad2b645`) before and after. `sync-bands.conf` now records `OLD_BASE=db3ed30` with the promoted SHAs, so nightly trials rebase from the genuine anchor. Script of record: the goal's `hidden_files/cleanup-promote.sh`.

**Standing ratification — GRANTED by Will, 2026-11-09** (supersedes the pending state recorded here since 2026-11-06): "unattended go-live for mutable.phext.io is fine - we will be watching his upstream repo for updates and automatically deploying them as a continuous CI/CD process." The mutable.phext.io deployment process is therefore a true continuous CI/CD loop: watch upstream, sync, compose (upstream + phext band until it lands + SSO band applied as deploy-time patches), promote per the mechanics above, verify, and deploy — unattended. The gate that remains is verification itself: a failed build/typecheck/test battery, a rebase or cherry-pick conflict, or a failed band-purity check BLOCKS the deploy and reports; automation proceeds only on a fully verified stack. This ratification is scoped to the mutable.phext.io deployment process; it is not a general license for unattended promotions elsewhere.
